{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '2984,3050p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '1380,1435p;1570,1638p'; cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; rg --files -uuu /tmp /Users/sto/workspace 2>/dev/null | rg '/r5-cljd.log\"'$'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"#### 14.1.1 Acceptance invariant and safepoints\n\nFor each ordered source/receiver pair in JVM, Node (CLJS), and Dart (CLJD),\na lower into a fresh task preserves the original logical stream identity,\nthe source-minted opaque kept position, the cursor-cell alias relation,\nand wait-set order. Test all nine pairs, including the three same-host\npairs as controls. Serializing a cursor must not translate it to a local\nanchor, offset, newly minted position, or copied stream. Host resource ids\nand seals are fresh; their equality across hosts is neither needed nor\npermitted as a substitute for logical cell identity.\n\nEvery reachable reference to one source cursor resource maps to one cell\nand one fresh receiver resource. Distinct cursor resources map to distinct\ncells even when their stream and position contents are equal. Advancing a\nshared cell is visible to its next waiter in the same polling round;\nadvancing one independent cell changes no other cell. Wait order comes\nfrom carried scheduler state, not map iteration or sorted cell ids.\n\n| Safepoint kind | Required resume evidence |\n|---|---|\n| Explicit park | No active wait; reachable cells keep their positions. |\n| Blocked read | Poll the remapped cell at its kept position. |\n| Blocked write | Retry the retained value on the original target. |\n| Sent FFI | Await the original response pair and kept response cell. |\n| Retained FFI | Retry the same request, id, args, and response cell. |\n| Ordinary/tail effectful call | Use the observed effect's pending shape. |\n| `:link-request` | Retry the retained link envelope and id verbatim. |\n| `:link-response` | Poll the original response cell; match the link id. |\n| `:install` | Preserve child phase and its waits, or refuse export. |\n| Halt | Carry a result; create no resumable frame or pending wait. |\n\nFor write-only rows the position obligation applies to all reachable\ncells, including response cells retained before the append. An outstanding\nFFI or link response uses the emitter's pair; a subsequent new operation\nuses the receiver's composed pair. A sent request is never reissued merely\nbecause a task moves. A retained request is not recomputed. Reflection\noutbound acceptance alone never wakes a writer: source acceptance is the\ncompletion evidence; an unknown append remains undischarged.\n\nStatic `:yin.safepoint/kinds` checks pc eligibility only. Dynamic reasons\nand pending variants come from observed parked records, wait entries, and\neffect outcomes, as ruled in `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 8. An\nexplicit park uses the approved no-wait shape. An effectful call must use\nits actual read, write, FFI, or link variant; an unspecified\n`:call-effect` shape cannot be invented from the static kind. An install\nname alone cannot reconstruct an active child; incomplete child evidence\nrefuses before publication.\n\n#### 14.1.2 State, wire, and lift/lower\n\nThe driver retains an export record containing the whole blocked task,\nordered waits, reachable parked records, fresh-name counter, isolated\nstore and module snapshots, and private resource graph. It also carries\nexplicit composition state for remote table entries and their lease\nroutes. Those routes keep the original streams served; this design does\nnot re-home a stream or promise export after its serving host disappears.\n\nNo cursor wire field is added. UCF 7.5.3 already carries\n`:yin.k/cells`, each cell's stream marker and `:yin.k/position`, and\n`:yin.k/cursor-ref` markers naming cells. Requirements name\n`:dao.stream.remote/v1` and every reachable stream identity. Cell ids\nare lift-local identities, never content hashes or position hashes.\nA cell mapping is shared by frame, waits, stores, closures, and parked\nrecords during one lift; separate encoders per root would erase aliasing.\n\nLift proceeds as follows:\n\n  1380\t\n  1381\t\n  1382\t(defn abandon-installs\n  1383\t  \"A composition gives up on every install in flight (the abandoned case\n  1384\t   of section 7.2, step 7, reaching section 7.3's `refused`): each child\n  1385\t   is dropped -- nothing from its store is published -- and every\n  1386\t   `:install` waiter is restored with the reason as the require's error,\n  1387\t   so the install entry is removed and a later require may try again. A\n  1388\t   state with no installs is unchanged. The dropped children's own link\n  1389\t   ids are never minted again by a state that carries `:origins` forward\n  1390\t   past them.\"\n  1391\t  [state reason]\n  1392\t  (reduce (fn [state module-name]\n  1393\t            (refuse-install state module-name\n  1394\t                            {:status :lost, :reason reason}))\n  1395\t          state\n  1396\t          (keys (:installs state))))\n  1397\t\n  1398\t\n  1399\t;; =============================================================================\n  1400\t;; FFI response routing\n  1401\t;; =============================================================================\n  1402\t;;\n  1403\t;; Several callers may read one call-out, and one VM may wait on several\n  1404\t;; response cells (its own call-out cursor, and the carried cells of\n  1405\t;; lowered retained calls). The generic sweep delivers positionally -- a\n  1406\t;; woken reader takes whatever value its cell holds next -- and\n  1407\t;; `yin.vm.ffi/call-result` could only check the id after the value was\n  1408\t;; consumed. The router matches before consuming: per response cell it\n  1409\t;; reads a bounded run of values, advances the cell on every `ok`, and\n  1410\t;; wakes only the waiter whose call id the response names. A response\n  1411\t;; for no live waiter is discarded with a diagnostic; `call-result`\n  1412\t;; stays the final validation of the matched envelope.\n  1413\t\n  1414\t(def ffi-response-budget\n  1415\t  \"How many values the router reads from one response cell per wait-set\n  1416\t   check. A run of other callers' responses longer than this is read\n  1417\t   across later checks.\"\n  1418\t  64)\n  1419\t\n  1420\t\n  1421\t(def ^:private ffi-diagnostics-cap\n  1422\t  \"How many unmatched-response diagnostics a VM keeps. A shared call-out\n  1423\t   carries every caller's responses, so skipping others' is ordinary\n  1424\t   traffic: the record is bounded, oldest dropped.\"\n  1425\t  64)\n  1426\t\n  1427\t\n  1428\t(defn- ffi-response-entry?\n  1429\t  \"True for a parked FFI response reader the router polls.\"\n  1430\t  [entry]\n  1431\t  (and (some? (ffi/response-call-id entry))\n  1432\t       (map? (:cursor-ref entry))))\n  1433\t\n  1434\t\n  1435\t(defn- ffi-skip\n  1570\t                  (make-woken-run-queue-entries state woken))))))\n  1571\t\n  1572\t\n  1573\t(defn check-wait-set\n  1574\t  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n  1575\t\n  1576\t   The sweep is `dao.stream.waitset/check` — this engine was that library's\n  1577\t   seed and is now its first consumer. Every entry is resolved to a live\n  1578\t   handle and an opaque cursor out of the store by `waitset-resolver` and\n  1579\t   polled with a synchronous `next` or `append!` — resolution happens per\n  1580\t   poll, so the stored entry itself never carries a handle. There is no\n  1581\t   transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only mechanism.\n  1582\t\n  1583\t   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n  1584\t   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n  1585\t   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values —\n  1586\t   the value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next —\n  1587\t   instead of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll\n  1588\t   cursor. An entry that stays waiting is retained in its stored,\n  1589\t   resource-id form.\n  1590\t\n  1591\t   Each woken result's `:status` is stamped onto its ready entry, where\n  1592\t   `terminal-resume-outcome` reads it: an outcome the immediate operation\n  1593\t   raises as an error fails the same way when the resume pops it — and a\n  1594\t   waitset diagnostic, an entry the sweep could not poll at all, raises\n  1595\t   there too, before any continuation is restored.\n  1596\t\n  1597\t   The link wait entries `require` adds (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.2) are\n  1598\t   polled first, by the engine rather than the stream sweep: a\n  1599\t   `:link-request` retries its envelope, a `:link-response` advances its\n  1600\t   own kept cursor to the response carrying its id, and an `:install`\n  1601\t   waits on its install child. The install children are then stepped in\n  1602\t   this same round (section 7.3), so a child that halts restores its\n  1603\t   waiters here; nothing about a child runs inside a restore.\n  1604\t\n  1605\t   The FFI response readers are routed next, also outside the stream\n  1606\t   sweep (`poll-ffi-responses`): a response wakes the reader whose call\n  1607\t   id it names, never whichever reader its cell reached first.\"\n  1608\t  [state]\n  1609\t  (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n  1610\t    (if (and (empty? wait-set) (empty? (:installs state)))\n  1611\t      state\n  1612\t      (let [state (poll-links state\n  1613\t                              (filterv link-entry? wait-set)\n  1614\t                              (remove link-entry? wait-set))\n  1615\t            wait-set (:wait-set state)\n  1616\t            state (poll-ffi-responses state\n  1617\t                                      (filterv ffi-response-entry? wait-set)\n  1618\t                                      (remove ffi-response-entry? wait-set))\n  1619\t            wait-set (:wait-set state)\n  1620\t            engine-polled? (some-fn link-entry? ffi-response-entry?)\n  1621\t            streams (filterv (complement engine-polled?) wait-set)\n  1622\t            {:keys [woken store], :as result}\n  1623\t            (waitset/check {:waiting streams}\n  1624\t                           waitset-resolver\n  1625\t                           (:resources state))\n  1626\t            v (assoc state\n  1627\t                     :resources store\n  1628\t                     :wait-set (into (:waiting (:waitset result))\n  1629\t                                     (filter engine-polled?)\n  1630\t                                     wait-set))]\n  1631\t        (update v\n  1632\t                :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n  1633\t                (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken))))))\n  1634\t\n  1635\t\n  1636\t(declare handle-effect resume-continuation)\n  1637\t\n  1638\t\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n/tmp/claude-501/-Users-sto-workspace-datomworld/ebd7e58a-319c-4c67-9a7b-8fecce46c256/scratchpad/r5-cljd.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/target/r5-cljd.log\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
